---
// A picture of the schematic staying true to the line: the engineer's change
// types itself into the rail in plain words, Spyke drafts the redline onto
// sheet E-402 (a photoeye lands on the spare terminal), a cursor arrives and
// clicks Approve, and the title block stamps REV C over to REV D. The markup
// is authored in its RESTING state — redline drafted, awaiting approval — so
// with no JS or reduced motion the mock sits at the most legible frame of the
// story. The script's classes (.play → .drafting → .drafted → .approved)
// replay it from the blank sheet. Nothing inside is focusable.
//
// The redline reuses the site's diff language: ink stays black, a soft green
// ring with a + badge marks the new circuit, drawn under the ink so the wires
// run through it; the retired SPARE marker gets a red strike and a − badge.
//
// The sheet shares the features' fiction: the guard door switch from REV C
// ("Add guard door interlock", yesterday) is already wired on row 2, and the
// new photoeye is the electrical side of the jam-detection change.
---

<section class="section" id="schematics">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head rise" data-rise>
      <h2>Schematics that keep up with the line</h2>
      <p>
        Wiring changes happen at the panel and never make it back to the drawing, until the
        schematic describes a machine that no longer exists. Describe the change in plain words —
        Spyke drafts the redline, you approve it, and the sheet is revised and versioned with the
        program.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="smock rise d1" data-rise data-live data-noreplay role="group" aria-label="Preview of Spyke drafting a schematic redline from a plain-language description">
      <div class="szoom">
      <div class="sheet-col">
        <div class="file-row">Drawings / E-402 — Conveyor Infeed</div>
